      <div class="inf-answer-grid">
        <div class="inf-answer-block"><h3>Infinity vs. Osaki</h3><p>Osaki generally delivers more feature density and stronger massage intensity — particularly through their Duo dual-engine lineup. Infinity counters with the distinctive Syner-D tandem feel, longer Flex-Track coverage, and a more stretch-oriented massage profile. If you're comparing these two brands directly, an in-person test of both is essential.</p></div>
        <div class="inf-answer-block"><h3>Infinity vs. Kyota</h3><p>Kyota is the consumer brand that originally developed Syner-D technology — Infinity licenses it. In practice, both deliver the tandem-roller feel, but Kyota applies it differently (Kyota M673 vs. Infinity Circadian, for example). HOMC carries both — we'll walk you through the exact differences in person.</p></div>
        <div class="inf-answer-block"><h3>Infinity vs. Ogawa</h3><p>Ogawa prioritizes design integration and a refined luxury aesthetic — their Master Drive Duo is a beautifully engineered chair. Infinity counters with more aggressive feature specifications at comparable prices. Choose Ogawa if design is a primary driver; Infinity if you want more technology per dollar.</p></div>
        <div class="inf-answer-block"><h3>Infinity vs. Bodyfriend</h3><p>Bodyfriend occupies a distinct premium position at $9K+ with their XD Pro roller module offering precision engineering. Infinity tends to pack more features at lower prices. Bodyfriend for design-led precision; Infinity for comprehensive features with proven Syner-D technology.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
